namespace Death {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @page movement-accuracy Non-Reforged movement accuracy
@brief The measured original movement model, and where this engine still differs from it

@tableofcontents

@m_class{m-noindent}

Reference for the **non-Reforged** movement model --- the mode that aims to reproduce the original
<b>Jazz Jackrabbit 2</b> rather than to play well by modern standards. Every figure attributed to the original
here was *measured*, not derived and not remembered, with the trajectory probe described in
@ref movement-accuracy-method. The constants themselves live in @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player, all prefixed
@cpp Legacy @ce, and every one of them is gated on @cpp !IsReforged() @ce.

Three fixes found along the way are the exception, because they are plain bugs in shared collision code
rather than differences between the two models, and all three are therefore live in **Reforged** as well:
the missing half-step of gravity in @ref Jazz2::Actors::ActorBase::TryStandardMovement()
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref movement-accuracy-framerate) @m_endspan, the slope search dropping an
actor off the end of a floor in @ref Jazz2::Actors::ActorBase::TryMoveSubstep()
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref movement-accuracy-ground) @m_endspan, and that same function's airborne
up-slope climb being switched off at exactly 45 degrees by a strict inequality
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref movement-accuracy-ground-slopejump) @m_endspan. None has a measured original
figure in it; each makes the code do what its own comment already claimed.

The **camera** is measured here too @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see @ref movement-accuracy-camera) @m_endspan but
is gated on its own preference, @ref Jazz2::PreferencesCache::EnableReforgedCamera, rather than on the
gameplay setting: it is local and purely visual, so it can reasonably differ from the physics a server
decides.

Four columns recur in most of the tables below @m_span{m-text m-dim} (a few one-off comparisons name their
own) @m_endspan:

Column | Meaning
------ | -------
<b>3.8.0</b> | The engine before this work @m_span{m-text m-dim} (commit `35eb085c`) @m_endspan. Hand-tuned by feel.
<b>Original</b> | **Jazz Jackrabbit 2** with **JJ2+** Beta v6.6h, measured at **70.021 ticks/s**.
<b>Now</b> | Current state, converted from the Original column.
<b>How far … was off</b> | Against the Now column, i.e. against the original converted to 60 Hz.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Why so many rows read "exact"
A constant that *is* the original's, scaled, cannot drift --- the only thing that could make it wrong is a
mistake in the measurement it came from, so those rows read exact by construction rather than by luck. The
informative rows are the **end-to-end** ones, where a whole manoeuvre is compared: jump height, sidekick
distance, kick distance, RF blast travel. Those are what catch a right constant applied the wrong way,
which is the failure this whole exercise kept running into.

@section movement-accuracy-units Units and the frame-rate conversion

The original runs at a fixed **70 ticks/s**; this engine has a variable tick rate with a **60 Hz** baseline
for @cpp timeMult @ce. Converting a per-tick figure between them:

-   **velocity** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (px/tick) @m_endspan × `70/60` = 1.166667
-   **acceleration** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (px/tick²) @m_endspan × `(70/60)²` = 1.361111
-   **duration** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (ticks) @m_endspan ÷ `70/60`

@m_class{m-noindent}

Scaling both velocity and acceleration exactly preserves jump height *and* real-time duration
simultaneously, so **no further tuning factor belongs in these constants**. 3.8.0 carried extra fudge
factors @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`×1.05` on ground speed, `×0.94` on vertical velocity, `×0.94²` on rise
gravity, `×0.977` on fall gravity) @m_endspan which are all gone.

The original works in **1/65536 px** internally, which is why its constants come out as clean fractions of
65536 --- a strong signal that a measurement landed on the real value rather than near it.

@section movement-accuracy-headline The headline finding

<b>The original applies one acceleration table to everything.</b> Ground and air are identical, and the
acceleration is applied in the direction of the *input* regardless of which way the player is moving. There
is no skid factor, no separate airborne rate, and no loss of momentum when the direction is flipped.

3.8.0 had a ground "skid" @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp LegacyRunBrakeScale @ce 0.4 /
@cpp LegacyWalkBrakeScale @ce 0.7) @m_endspan and, at various points during this work, several different
airborne models. All of them were inventions, and all are deleted.

@section movement-accuracy-ground Ground movement

Quantity | 3.8.0 | Original (70 Hz) | Now (60 Hz) | How far 3.8.0 was off | How far Now is off
-------- | ----- | ---------------- | ----------- | --------------------- | ------------------
Walk speed cap | 4.900 | 4.0 | 4.6667 | +5.0% | exact
Dash speed cap | 9.800 | 16.0 | 18.6667 | **−47.5%** | exact
Acceleration, no Run | 0.380 | 0.18311 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`12000/65536`) @m_endspan | 0.24924 | **+52.5%** | exact
Acceleration, Run held | 0.380 | 0.36621 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`24000/65536`) @m_endspan | 0.49847 | −23.7% | exact
Deceleration, walking | 0.154 | 0.12207 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`8000/65536`) @m_endspan | 0.16616 | −7.3% | exact
Deceleration, in dash state | --- | 0.42725 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`28000/65536`) @m_endspan | 0.58155 | *(absent)* | exact
Turn-around skid multiplier | ×0.4 / ×0.7 | **none** | 1.0 | *(invented)* | exact

@m_class{m-noindent}

Note 3.8.0 used the *same* acceleration whether or not Run was held; the original doubles it. That is why
walking felt too twitchy @m_span{m-text m-dim} (+52%) @m_endspan and dashing too sluggish
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (−24%) @m_endspan at the same time.

<b>Full stop times</b> independently confirm the deceleration model --- these were measured, then found to
agree with the constants, rather than fitted to them:

From | Original | Predicted by the applied constants
---- | -------- | ----------------------------------
Walk cap | 33 ticks @m_span{m-text m-dim} (0.47 s) @m_endspan | `4.0 / 0.12207` = 32.8 ✔
Dash cap | 49 ticks @m_span{m-text m-dim} (0.70 s) @m_endspan | 16 grace ticks + 33 ✔

@subsection movement-accuracy-ground-dash The dash is a timed state, not a cap

<b>Not present in 3.8.0 at all.</b> Holding Run raises both the cap and the acceleration. Letting go does
<em>not</em> bleed the speed off: the player keeps the full dash speed for **16 ticks**
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (13.71 of ours) @m_endspan and the speed is then clamped straight back to the walk
cap in a single tick --- measured @cpp 16.0000 @ce → @cpp 4.0000 @ce in one tick, in mid-air exactly as on
the ground. See @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyDashGraceTicks.

@subsection movement-accuracy-ground-applied Applied-movement cap, the subtlest difference

<b>The original caps applied movement at 8 px/tick on *both* axes.</b> A dash reports an @cpp xSpeed @ce of 16
but only ever *travels* 8. The excess is momentum, not motion, and it still counts wherever the speed is
<em>read</em> --- which is what makes a dashing jump launch at −14 while moving no faster than a capped run.

3.8.0 had this on the vertical axis only @m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp LegacyRiseSpeedCap @ce, already
correct at 8) @m_endspan. Adding the horizontal half was worth 300--570 px of error per dash scenario.

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par Consequence for feel
The dash's actual ground speed is now *half* what 3.8.0 delivered, while its reported speed is double.
Both are correct. This is one of the three changes most likely to feel wrong despite measuring right.

@subsection movement-accuracy-ground-slopes Slopes

<b>Measured: slopes have no effect on horizontal speed or acceleration whatsoever.</b> Walking downhill
@cpp xs @ce stays at exactly 4.0 and x advances exactly 4/tick while y drops 4/tick; uphill is identical,
with no slowdown. The acceleration curve is unchanged on an incline @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`10.8032` at the
same tick both ways) @m_endspan. This engine already behaved correctly here --- no change was needed.

@subsection movement-accuracy-ground-slopejump Jumping into a slope, where 45 degrees is exactly the wrong number

Walking or dashing *along* a slope needed no change @m_span{m-text m-dim} (see above) @m_endspan, but
jumping **into** one did, and the failure sat on an exact equality rather than anywhere near it.

@ref Jazz2::Actors::ActorBase::TryMoveSubstep()'s airborne branch retries a blocked horizontal step with an
increasing upward offset, which is what lets a mostly-horizontal move ride up a gentle incline instead of
stopping at its foot. It was gated on @cpp abs(stepX) > abs(stepY) @ce. A dashing jump travels 8 px across
while rising at the applied cap of **8**, so against a 45-degree face those two are not merely close, they
are equal --- and a strict @cpp > @ce switches the climb off at precisely the angle it exists for. What
follows is automatic: the loop below grinds the horizontal step down until something fits, the leftover is
under 30% of the step, @cpp HitWall @ce is reported, and @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::OnHitWall() zeroes the
horizontal speed.

Scenario | Original | Before | After
-------- | -------- | ------ | -----
`sl_up45_jhold` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (dash + jump) @m_endspan | 16.0000 throughout | **0.9977** | 18.6667 throughout
`sl_up45_run` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (dash, no jump) @m_endspan | 16.0000 | 18.6667 | 18.6667
`sl_up45_jwalk` @m_span{m-text m-dim} (walk + jump) @m_endspan | 4.0000 | 4.6667 | 4.6667

@m_class{m-block m-danger}

@par The first scenarios written for this measured nothing, and agreed while doing it
`sl_up_jhold` and its two siblings jump into the level's *designated* up-slope --- which is 6 tiles wide by
5 tall, about 40 degrees. A jump rising at 45 is **steeper than that face**, so it clears the slope instead
of meeting it, and both games sail up keeping their speed. Three scenarios, two games, full agreement, and
not one of them touched the rule. The case only appears against a true 45-degree face, and the level has one
in a place nobody would look for it: the long *downhill* slope at tiles (193,41)-(210,57) is 17 by 16, so
running **leftwards** up it is the geometry required. Read this next to
@ref movement-accuracy-objects-poles' lesson about counting how many fit points are actually unclamped ---
same failure, different disguise: a scenario that cannot reach the boundary reports agreement, not silence.

@subsection movement-accuracy-ground-gaps Crossing a gap, and the "ledge hop" that never existed

This engine had an *original-style ledge hop*: running off a ledge gave a small upward boost so the player
would clear a small gap instead of dropping into it. Measured over gaps of one, two, three and four tiles,
the original gives **no such boost**. Running off the edge, @cpp ys @ce goes straight into the fall gravity
--- 0, 0.125, 0.250, 0.375, … --- and a four-tile gap is crossed having dropped under 10 px, landing on the
far edge purely because 8 px of travel a tick outruns the fall. A walk drops far enough to miss the far
edge of a three-tile gap and falls in, which is the same arithmetic rather than a separate rule. So gap
clearing needs no code at all: the applied speed cap and the fall gravity already produce it, and both are
measured exactly. The boost is gone, and with it the last two hand-tuned scale factors in the whole model.

@m_class{m-block m-danger}

@par What the boost was hiding
With it removed, the player could not cross a **two-tile** gap even at full dash --- the original clears
four. The cause was in @ref Jazz2::Actors::ActorBase::TryMoveSubstep(): the slope search tries candidate
angles from the most downward upwards and takes the first free one, which follows a downhill slope
correctly, but over a gap *every* candidate is free, so the first one won and the actor was dropped by the
whole search range the instant the floor ended. Since that range grows with the horizontal step, running
faster dropped the actor **deeper** rather than carrying it further --- 17 px in one tick at dash speed,
below the far edge, into the wall. A downward candidate is now only taken when there is ground under it,
which is what "following a slope" was supposed to mean.

That fix was worth two tiles of reach, and a sweep over gaps of one to six tiles - each with a full run-up,
so every gap is met at true top speed - then exposed a second one. The engine was landing a whole gap short
of the original at **both** speeds, because the fall matched tick for tick but the **landing** did not:
how far below a ledge's surface the player may arrive and still be put on top of it, rather than caught by
its side.

The original's allowance follows from its own four boundary cases. It clears a two-tile walk, arriving
6.25 px below the far edge, and a five-tile dash, arriving 18.1 px below; it fails a three-tile walk
(20.25 px) and a six-tile dash (27.6 px). So the allowance sits between **18.1 and 20.25 px** --- five
eighths of a tile, and @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyLandingTolerance takes the round 20.

This engine's reach was whatever the horizontal sub-step happened to be, about 6 px: the airborne branch of
@ref Jazz2::Actors::ActorBase::TryMoveSubstep() already climbs when a horizontal step is blocked - that is
what lets a sidekick ride up a gentle slope - but only by @cpp abs(stepX) + 2.5 @ce. A descending actor was
therefore caught by the ledge's side. The allowance is opt-in per actor
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (@cpp ActorBase::_landingTolerance @ce, 0 by default) @m_endspan and only the
non-Reforged player sets it, since that is the only thing it was measured on. With it, all twelve scenarios
agree with the original:

Approach | Original | Ours
-------- | -------- | ----
Walking @m_span{m-text m-dim} (4 px/tick) @m_endspan | clears 1 and 2, falls into 3 | same
Dashing @m_span{m-text m-dim} (8 px/tick applied) @m_endspan | clears 1 to 5, falls into 6 | same

@m_class{m-noindent}

One residual: after landing from a crossing, the original keeps more speed than this engine does, so a run
that takes the four-tile gap and then meets the five-tile one clears it there and not here. Each gap
approached with its own run-up matches.

@section movement-accuracy-air Jumping and air movement

Quantity | 3.8.0 | Original (70 Hz) | Now (60 Hz) | How far 3.8.0 was off | How far Now is off
-------- | ----- | ---------------- | ----------- | --------------------- | ------------------
Jump launch, standing | −10.967 | −10.0 | −11.6667 | −6.0% | exact
Launch bonus per unit of @cpp xSpeed @ce | @cpp max(0, abs(xs) − 4) * 0.3 @ce | @cpp abs(xs) / 4 @ce, **no threshold** | @cpp abs(xs) / 4 @ce | wrong shape | exact
Rise gravity, jump held | 0.45101 | 0.375 | 0.51042 | −11.6% | exact
Rise gravity, jump released | *(speed clamped to −4.387)* | 0.875 | 1.19097 | **wrong mechanism** | exact
Rise gravity, released, last px/tick | *(as above)* | 0.625 | 0.85069 | *(absent)* | exact
Fall gravity | 0.16623 | 0.125 | 0.17014 | −2.3% | exact
Underwater gravity | 0.01879 | 0.015625 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`1024/65536`) @m_endspan | 0.02127 | −11.6% | exact
Applied rise cap | 9.3333 | 8.0 | 9.3333 | correct | exact
Terminal fall speed | 13.160 | 12.0 | 14.0 | −6.0% | exact
Air acceleration | 0.380 + skid | same as ground | same as ground | *(invented)* | exact
Standing jump height @m_span{m-text m-dim} (end to end) @m_endspan | *(not measured)* | 132.0 px | 128.2 px | --- | −2.9%

@m_class{m-noindent}

<b>Releasing jump changes the rise gravity</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (0.375 → 0.875) @m_endspan from that tick
onward; it does not clamp the speed. That is why a tap hop has its own arc in the original rather than a
clipped one, and it is what gives Spaz's double jump its hold-duration control for free.

<b>Measured arcs</b>, all original: standing jump 132.0 px @m_span{m-text m-dim} (4.13 tiles) @m_endspan, apex
at +27 ticks, airtime ~73 ticks. Tap jump 72.9 px. Walking jump 153.4 px, dx 312 px. Dashing jump 217.0 px,
dx 752 px.

@subsection movement-accuracy-air-rise The rise, tick by tick

Read off `ap_r01`..`ap_r30` --- thirty standing jumps released one tick apart, which is the only family that
samples the apex on purpose rather than incidentally. In the original's own units, once per tick:

@code{.cpp}
if (ys < 0) {                                   // still rising
    brake = held ? 0.25
                 : (abs(ys) > 1.0 ? 0.75        // ... and the released rate eases off
                                  : 0.5);       //     for the last pixel per tick
    if (ys + brake > 0.125) ys = 0;             // the rise ENDS rather than overshooting
    else                    ys += brake;
}
ys += 0.125;                                    // gravity, every tick, rising or falling
y  += max(ys, -8);                              // the applied rise cap
@endcode

@m_class{m-noindent}

This reproduces **all thirty peaks to 0.01 px** --- 58.87, 63.62, 68.00 … saturating at 132.00 from
`ap_r27`, where the release comes later than the apex and stops mattering. Three things in it are worth
naming, because each was wrong or missing before and none is visible in a single arc:

-   **The released rate is two rates.** 0.875 while the rise is faster than one pixel per tick, 0.625 below
    it. Measured either side of that threshold: the step is 0.875 at @cpp ys @ce = −1.125 and 0.625 at
    −1.000. The **held** rate has no such band, on 379 samples.
-   **The rise never overshoots by more than one tick of gravity.** From −0.375 a released rise lands on
    +0.250 and keeps it, but from −0.250 and −0.125 it lands on +0.125, not the +0.375 and +0.500 the
    deceleration alone would give. This is not a one-tick detail: an overshoot becomes a fixed offset on the
    entire descent that follows.
-   **The applied cap is what makes the first six ticks flat.** They travel exactly −8.0 while @cpp ys @ce
    reads −10.000 down to −8.125, which is worth 6.375 px of the 132 --- and a model without it overshoots
    every peak in the sweep by exactly that.

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par Where it pays off, and where it does not
Not on the **peak** of a short jump: the mean absolute peak error across the sweep moved only from 2.23 px
to 2.19 px, because the last tick or two of a rise is worth very little height. It pays off on everything
that happens *after* a released rise, because the old overshoot was not a one-tick artefact --- carrying up
to +0.94 of spurious fall speed out of the apex offsets the entire descent. `sp_dj_r50_d25` went from
<b>11.2 px short to 1.1 px over</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (144.6 against 143.5) @m_endspan, `sp_dj_r50_d29`
from 6.4 short to 1.9 over, and the three `fu_col_*` all improved, their exits decaying at the released
rate. All thirty `ap_r*` now score **exactly 0.0 px/s**.

What it does not fix is the standing jump, still 128.0 px against 132.0. **That residual is the time step,
not the model**: at 60 Hz a frame covers 7/6 of the original's ticks, so the flat capped part of the rise is
sampled 6 times where the original samples it 7, and a coarser sample of the same capped ascent covers less
ground. The sweep shows it plainly --- the error follows the release tick between +3.0 and −4.0 px, and
pairs of adjacent release ticks collapse onto one frame @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`ap_r03`/`ap_r04` both give
70.96, `ap_r17`/`ap_r18` both 119.00) @m_endspan which no change to the rise rule can separate.

@subsection movement-accuracy-air-turn Air turn-around, the one that took five attempts

Since ground and air share one table, turning around in the air is symmetric and keeps its momentum, which
is what makes it slow. Four different invented models were tried and rejected before measurement settled
it.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par This contradicts a widely held belief
Turning at the apex does **not** return the player to the launch point in the original. Measured landing
offsets, *forward* of takeoff: walking jump and turn **+114.8 px**, dashing and turn **+548 px**
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (a no-turn walking jump lands at +312) @m_endspan. The air acceleration is simply too
weak --- a full reversal takes 43.7 ticks walking and 87.4 dashing. Chasing the belief instead of the
measurement cost several rounds of work.

@section movement-accuracy-objects Objects

Quantity | 3.8.0 | Original (70 Hz) | Now (60 Hz) | How far 3.8.0 was off | How far Now is off
-------- | ----- | ---------------- | ----------- | --------------------- | ------------------
Spring, red --- both axes | 7.090 vert / 11.111 horiz | **16.0** | 18.6667 | **−62% / −40%** | exact
Spring, green --- both axes | 7.847 / 11.111 | **24.0** | 28.0 | **−72% / −60%** | exact
Spring, blue --- both axes | 9.774 / 11.111 | **32.0** | 37.3333 | **−74% / −70%** | exact
Vertical pole launch | @cpp 4*sign + entry*1.4 @ce, ×2.5 | @cpp 15.625 + abs(entry) @ce | @cpp 18.229 + abs(entry) @ce | wrong shape | exact
Horizontal pole launch | @cpp 10*sign + entry*0.2 @ce + force 10 | @cpp clamp(3 * abs(entry), 8, 20) @ce | @cpp clamp(3 * abs(entry), 9.333, 23.333) @ce | wrong shape | exact
Vine cap, no Run | 2.0 | 2.0 | 2.3333 | −14.3% | exact
Vine cap, Run held | 3.2 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (×1.6) @m_endspan | 4.0 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (**×2**) @m_endspan | 4.6667 | −31.4% | exact
Push speed @m_span{m-text m-dim} (solid object) @m_endspan | 0.6 <b>× @cpp timeMult @ce</b> | 0.375 | 0.4375 | frame-rate dependent | exact

@subsection movement-accuracy-objects-springs Springs set the speed outright

No formula, no added external force, and **only the colour matters --- the same figure on both axes**.
3.8.0 used one shared strength for every horizontal spring and a completely different scale for vertical
ones; neither matched. The colours differ in how *long* they push, not how fast the player visibly moves,
because the applied cap holds all three to 8 px/tick.

@m_class{m-block m-danger}

@par The heights that "did not reconcile" were two separate probe bugs
For a long time the spring *launches* matched exactly while the resulting *heights* did not, and it was
written off as test-level geometry. It was neither. Two things were wrong, and both were in the harness or
in a limit rather than in the movement.

The first: **a spring left under the player's feet fires during the settle.** The probe removed the
previous scenario's spawned object just before the run rather than at the reset, so the player was launched
while the next scenario was still lining up, and that scenario's first logged tick was already 60 px up
with a third of its speed spent. This affected the second and third spring in the row and *both* games ---
which is why green appeared to rise less than red despite the stronger launch, the figure that made the
whole thing look impossible. Cleaned up at the reset, the original's rises come out **267.5, 438.4 and
609.0 px** for red, green and blue: linear in launch speed, +170.7 px for each +8 of it. The numbers that
had been quoted for green and blue, 326.8 and 377.9, were the contamination.

@m_class{m-block m-danger}

@par An internal speed limit that sat below a launch it had to carry
The spring *launches* matched exactly from the day they were measured, and the resulting *heights* did not
--- for a long time written off as test-level geometry. It was neither. @cpp _verticalSpeedLimit @ce was
32.0, and a blue spring's own 32 px/tick becomes **37.33** in this engine's units, so the limit truncated
the launch on the tick it fired: 5.33 of speed gone before gravity had touched it. A spring feeding a pole
then entered the pole 5 short and came out 380 px low. The limit only governs how long a launch keeps
rising at the applied cap of 8, so a generous value costs nothing in travel. A 64 was tried first --- it
cleared every launch, but then never clamped the pole either, which the original does clamp --- so it is
now the original's own limit of 32 px/tick, i.e. **37.33** here, which is exactly a blue spring's launch:
a spring passes through untouched while a pole is still clamped the way the original clamps it. The same
figure bounds the horizontal axis, where the original uses it too.

With both fixed, and both sides measured clean:

Spring | Original | Ours | Difference
------ | -------- | ---- | ----------
Red @m_span{m-text m-dim} (16) @m_endspan | 267.5 px | 282.1 | +14.6 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (5.5%) @m_endspan
Green @m_span{m-text m-dim} (24) @m_endspan | 438.4 px | 452.5 | +14.1 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (3.2%) @m_endspan
Blue @m_span{m-text m-dim} (32) @m_endspan | 609.0 px | 597.3 | −11.7 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (1.9%) @m_endspan

@m_class{m-noindent}

The **two ticks a spring holds its launch speed** before gravity starts --- which this engine does not
reproduce --- account for the blue deficit exactly: the simple model of a capped rise decaying at 0.375
predicts 256, 426.7 and 597.3, and the original sits about 11.5 px above all three while ours lands on the
model precisely for blue. Adding the hold would therefore fix blue and make red and green worse by the same
amount, so it is not the whole story and is deliberately not applied on its own. What is left is a couple
of percent that grows as the launch weakens, which has the shape of integration detail near the apex
rather than a missing rule.

@m_class{m-block m-danger}

@par A horizontal spring must not move the player
@cpp OnHitSpring() @ce pulled the player **halfway towards the spring's own centre** before assigning the
speed. The original does not move them at all: dropped down a one-tile shaft onto a blue horizontal spring,
its y reads 1428.001, 1428.126, 1428.376 over the launch tick and the two after it --- creeping only as
gravity rebuilds @cpp ys @ce from zero. The snap put ours 10 px lower on the tick the spring fired.

Every scenario built out of props missed this, for a structural reason: they all *walk* into a horizontal
spring, at the height it already sits at, where the midpoint of the two positions **is** the position. Only
something *falling* onto one is affected, and it took a chain in shipped level geometry to produce that ---
Diamondus 3's west end, where a fall down a shaft lands on a horizontal spring and is thrown along a
corridor. There the 10 px decided whether the player cleared a wall one tile up and escaped the chain, or
hit it, dropped onto a horizontal pole with no speed left, and was thrown back to the spring for ever. That
scenario scored **204.5 px/s before the fix and 0 after it**. Reforged keeps the snap.

@subsection movement-accuracy-objects-floatup Float-up areas hold a speed, they do not push

An @ref EventType::AreaFloatUp assigns @cpp ys = -8 @ce **every tick the player is inside it**. Not an
acceleration, not an external force, and no ceiling to accelerate towards: measured inside a solid ten-tile
column of them, the original reads exactly −8.0000 on every one of the 43 ticks in the field and only starts
decaying once the player leaves. It also needs no jump and lifts a *grounded* player straight off the floor
--- pressing nothing gives a trace identical to jumping, tick for tick.

3.8.0 | Original (70 Hz) | Now (60 Hz) | How far 3.8.0 was off | How far Now is off
----- | ---------------- | ----------- | --------------------- | ------------------
@cpp force -2*g*timeMult @ce | @cpp ys = -8 @ce while inside | @cpp ys = -9.333 @ce | wrong shape | exact

@m_class{m-noindent}

The continuous force never assigns a real upward speed, so it oscillated around zero instead of lifting.
Riding the test level's diagonal ladder of them gained **149.8 px against the original's 520.3** --- the
horizontal travel matched to 0.2 px the whole way, so the player crossed the ladder without climbing it.
It is now 520.7.

**And there is no gravity on a tick spent inside the field**, which is a second rule and had to be
implemented separately. The original travels exactly the 8.0 it assigns; ours travelled 7.79 of the
original's units because the velocity-Verlet half-step is folded into the move before the applied rise cap
gets to clamp it. Every *other* launch is immune to that by accident: they are all assigned above the cap,
so the half-step is clamped straight back off and the travel comes out at the cap regardless. A float area
is the one source whose assignment is **equal** to the cap --- @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyFloatUpSpeed
and @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyRiseSpeedCap are both 8 --- so it has no headroom and loses the whole
half-step. @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::GetGravityModifier() now returns zero while the player is inside one,
which covers both the half-step before the move and the application after it.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par The sawtooth is the geometry, not the rule
Read off a *ladder* rather than a column, the same mechanic looks like a repeating impulse: @cpp ys @ce
falls to −8, decays at the rise gravity, and is slammed back to −8 about a tick after it turns positive with
jump held --- but six ticks after, with jump released, and then held at −8 for five ticks. None of that is
a rule. The ladder's tiles are discrete and unevenly spaced, so the player keeps leaving and re-entering
them and the ascent decays in the gaps between. The mean climb is half the assignment, ~3.9 px/tick, and
because a sawtooth from −8 to 0 has the same mean whatever its slope, **the decay rate barely changes the
climb at all** --- which is why holding jump versus releasing it is worth only 7% here. Two scenario
families exist for exactly this reason: `fu_*` rides the level's ladder, `fu_col_*` sits in a probe-placed
column where the geometry cannot confound the timing. The column is what the model came from.

@subsection movement-accuracy-objects-tube A sucker tube re-arms while you are inside it

The tube assigns a speed on each axis outright, and both games agree on that number exactly --- a tube set
to 8 reads a flat 8.0000, one set to 20 reads 20.0000. What had to be worked out is how the hold *ends*,
and a single tube tile cannot answer it: the player leaves the 32 px tile in four ticks whatever happens, so
"a timer" and "still inside a tile" predict the same thing.

Two measurements separate them. A **30-tile row** of tubes carries the player the whole length in both
games, so the speed is re-assigned every tick they are inside one --- not applied once. And the two
single-tube scenarios hold for **different** lengths: 20 ticks at 8 px/tick, 18 at 20 px/tick. That
difference is exactly the two extra ticks the slower tube keeps the player inside its own tile, which puts
the window at @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyTubeControlTime = **16 ticks, re-armed while inside**.

The window suppresses horizontal friction but **not** gravity: a tube firing straight up holds its speed
only while the player is still in the tile, and the ascent decays at the ordinary rise gravity from there.
That is why the vertical case looked like a 3-tick hold while the horizontal one looked like 19.

Quantity | 3.8.0 | Original (70 Hz) | Now (60 Hz) | How far 3.8.0 was off | How far Now is off
-------- | ----- | ---------------- | ----------- | --------------------- | ------------------
Speed assigned | @cpp xs = param @ce | same | same | exact | exact
Hold window | 10 ticks, fixed | **16, re-armed inside** | 13.71 frames | −38% | exact
Speed on release | kept in full | **clamped to the walk cap** | clamped | wrong shape | exact
Speed on release, Run held | kept in full | **clamped to 16** | clamped | wrong shape | exact

@m_class{m-noindent}

That last row is inert here and is stated anyway: a tube carries 8 px/tick, so a clamp to 16 never bites and
the ride simply continues. It matters because the clamp is **one rule shared with the accelerating belt and
the sidekick** --- @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyCarryExitRunSpeed --- and clamping to the walk cap
regardless, which is what this engine did, cost `tb_right_run` a fifth of its distance: 166.4 px against the
original's 222.9, for holding a key that should if anything help. It is 219.9 now.

@m_class{m-noindent}

The release clamp is the one that showed: a tube set to 20 px/tick carried the player **1066.9 px** before,
against the original's 407.5, because the whole tube speed was handed back instead of being cut to 4. Its
Wait Time parameter is still a @cpp TODO @ce, and at a value of 3 changes nothing in either game.

Where in the tile the tube *holds* the player was wrong by 7 px, reported as a centring problem and measured
as one. Riding `tb_row`, the original sits at @cpp y @ce = **1327.000** for the whole ride, unchanging ---
15 px into the tile at row 41, a pixel above its centre. This engine snapped to 8, a quarter of the way
down. The horizontal branch had always used the tile centre; only the vertical one was off. It is
@ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyTubeSnapY now and the ride matches to the digit.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par About 1.8 px of that is not the tube's
The two games' player origins differ by roughly that much for the same standing position --- 1330.5 against
1328.7 resting on a floor, and the same gap the moment the player leaves the tube --- and nothing anywhere
in this work corrects for it. Matching the tube's measured number leaves the residual in place rather than
inventing a correction that would then have to be applied to every other absolute position too.

@subsection movement-accuracy-objects-flycarrot The flying carrot is two accelerations

Reported as the mechanic this engine had least of, and that was right. It is also the second thing on this
page that **scripted input cannot reach**: with the carrot collected and @cpp jjPLAYER.fly @ce reading
FLYCARROT for a whole run, eighty ticks of scripted Jump move the original's player not at all --- not even
an ordinary jump --- while Left and Right get through normally. The flight reads the raw key, exactly as the
rev-up does, so it was measured from a hand-played recording of 3871 ticks of continuous flying.

Quantity | Original | Ours before
-------- | -------- | -----------
Rising | **Up** held, accelerating 0.25 px/tick to the 32 cap | no rise at all; Jump ignored
Still rising, **Jump** held | brakes at **0.25** | ---
Still rising, nothing held | brakes at **0.75**, clamped at zero | ---
Then falling | accelerates 0.0625 to a terminal of 12 | hovers; no gravity whatever
Climb actually travelled | **8 px/tick**, the ordinary applied cap | ---
**Down** held | a fixed **1.0625 px/tick**, assigned outright | fixed 3.429 px/tick
Horizontally | ordinary ground movement --- 4 walking, 16 with Run | 4
Duration | until a Fly Off area or a death | **ten seconds**

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par There are three downward rates, and using one of them flies far too high
The braking rate that ends a climb is **not** the rate the flight then falls at. Releasing Up at the 32 cap
stops the climb in 44 ticks; treating that as the fall rate takes 512 and carries the player most of a level
upwards first, which is exactly how it felt before this was separated out. The first implementation here had
one rate and was reported as flying too fast up --- correctly. It is also not
@ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyRiseGravityReleased, the ordinary released-jump decay: that is 0.875, and
the recording contains 297 steps of 0.75 and none of 0.875.

The **travel** is capped at 8 px/tick throughout, the same applied cap a spring or a dash gets, so the speed
number running on to 32 buys height only in how long the climb sustains itself --- the trace shows @cpp ys @ce
reading −27 while @cpp y @ce still moves exactly 8 px a tick. That cap was already in place here and needed
nothing; it is what makes the climb rate match at 8.035 against 8.000.

The accelerations are as clean as measurements on this page get: 934 ticks of the first recording step by
exactly −0.25 and 1243 by exactly +0.0625.

The brake has a **held and a released form**, exactly as an ordinary jump does and with its own two numbers:
**0.25** with Jump down against 0.75 with nothing. The second recording shows both inside one manoeuvre ---
a jump taken from the ground *into* the carrot decays at the ordinary 0.375 for the six ticks it is still a
jump, and at 0.25 from the tick the flight animation takes over, for thirty ticks with Jump held throughout.
Jumping while already flying launches at −10 as usual.

And **Down** is a fourth rate that is not an acceleration at all: it *assigns* 1.0625 px/tick and holds it.
The recording snaps from a 3.3125 px/tick fall straight to it on the tick Down goes down, then reads 1.0625
for **734 consecutive ticks** without varying; letting go resumes the ordinary fall acceleration from
wherever the speed had got to. That is what "descends slowly on Down at a rate that is not the copter's"
turns out to mean --- @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyCopterDescentSpeed is 1.0078, close enough to sound
alike and far enough apart to measure.

Ours now reproduces all four: 0.2502 against 0.25 with Jump held, 0.7507 against 0.75 with nothing, 0.0625
exactly for the fall, a flat 1.0625 on Down, and the crossing from rise to fall clamping to exactly zero
rather than overshooting, as the original's does.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Neither probe can log the Up key
JJ2+ exposes no @cpp keyUp @ce, so those 934 rising ticks appear in the trace with **every logged key at
zero**. A rise with no visible input is what Up looks like here, and reading it as an automatic bob was the
first wrong conclusion drawn from this recording. **Down is a different story**: a second recording holds it
for 734 unbroken ticks with the column reading 1 throughout, so @cpp keyDown @ce *is* surfaced and the first
recording simply had none in it. Only Up is invisible --- which meant the flight's own descent key could be
measured normally once somebody pressed it.

@par The event that was supposed to end it did not
@ref Jazz2::EventType::AreaFlyOff cancelled @cpp Modifier::Airboard @ce only, never the carrot. That went
unnoticed while the flight had a ten-second timer of its own; removing the timer without it would have left
the player flying for the rest of the level.

@subsection movement-accuracy-objects-slide The slide tile swaps the brake

A `MODIFIER_SLIDE` tile adds nothing to the movement and changes no speed. It replaces the deceleration
that applies with no direction held, so a player who lets go coasts further --- which is the whole of what
it does, and why it was missed: @cpp EventType::ModifierSlide @ce was converted from the JJ2 event and then
read by **nothing at all**.

Its 2-bit Strength parameter scales both brakes, linearly, and all four values were measured:

Strength | Walk brake | Dash brake
-------- | ---------- | ----------
--- @m_span{m-text m-dim} (no slide) @m_endspan | 8000 | 28000
0 | 4000 | 12000
1 | 3200 | 9500
2 | 2400 | 7000
3 | 1600 | 4500

@m_class{m-noindent}

All in 65536ths of a pixel per tick squared, and both columns are straight lines: @cpp 4000 - 800*s @ce and
@cpp 12000 - 2500*s @ce. Note that strength 0 halves the walk brake but takes the dash brake to 3/7 rather
than 1/2, so a single "slide factor" applied to both would be wrong --- and measuring only strengths 0 and 3
would have given ratios of 2.5 and 2.67 and fitted no divisor at all. Four points for a four-value table.

Travel after letting go, against the original:

Strength | Original | Ours | Before
-------- | -------- | ---- | ------
0 | 645.7 px | 653.5 | 587.8 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (no slide at all) @m_endspan
1 | 678.5 px | 686.2 | ---
2 | 733.1 px | 740.7 | ---

@m_class{m-noindent}

The residual ~1.2% is the same discretisation the ordinary deceleration carries; a walk that ends in a stop
matches to 2%. See @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacySlideWalkDecel.

@subsection movement-accuracy-objects-hforce Wind and belts move the player, the accelerating belt drives them

Three event families push the player sideways without being touched, and none of them had ever been
measured. Two of the three move the player **by position** --- the original's @cpp xs @ce reads 0.0000 for
the whole time a player is being blown or carried along --- and the third works on the speed instead.

Quantity | 3.8.0 | Original (70 Hz) | Now (60 Hz) | How far 3.8.0 was off | How far Now is off
-------- | ----- | ---------------- | ----------- | --------------------- | ------------------
Wind, per unit of strength | 0.7 px/tick | **0.5** | 0.5833 | **+20%** | 0.2%
Belt, per unit | 0.7 px/tick | **1.0** | 1.1667 | **−30%** | 0.1%
Belt strength, no parameter | 3 | **2** | 2 | +50% | exact
Accelerating belt, per unit | @cpp xs += 0.1 @ce, uncapped | **1.5 px/tick, held** | 1.75 | wrong shape | exact
Acc. belt step, per unit | @cpp 0.1 @ce/frame, unscaled | **0.5 px/tick^2** | 0.6806 | frame-rate dependent | exact
Acc. belt strength, no parameter | 3 | **4** | 4 | −25% | exact

@m_class{m-noindent}

Each figure is fitted on two strengths, not one --- the mistake @ref movement-accuracy-objects-poles was
recorded with. Wind at 8 gives 4.0 px/tick and at 4 gives 2.0, so it is linear through the origin. A belt at
8 gives 8.0 and a parameterless one 2.0, which is what pins its default at 2 rather than 3: a single point
would have fitted "strength 4 × 0.5" just as well and been wrong everywhere else. **Wind and belts share an
event type in this engine but not a strength** --- a belt pulls exactly twice as hard as a wind of the same
number.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par The event name does not decide which way a wind blows
`MODIFIER_WIND_LEFT` with a *positive* parameter blows the player **right** in the original, exactly as this
engine's converter reads it. The parameter is signed --- 248 is the negative of 8 --- and the two event ids
differ only in which sign a level editor offers by default. This was written up as a likely conversion bug
before it was measured; it is not one. `wd_stand_l` and `wd_stand_r` in the harness are the pair that
settled it, and they are deliberately kept even though they now produce identical native events.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par The accelerating belt, and why it took four measurements
It drives @cpp xs @ce to @cpp 1.5 x strength @ce over three ticks and holds it there, and each of the four
things that were wrong only became visible once the one before it was fixed:
-   Reforged adds a fixed step per frame with **no ceiling**, unscaled by @cpp timeMult @ce, so it crawled
    to the original's speed over 45 frames and then sailed past it.
-   At a strength of 8 the target is 12 px/tick, straight through the applied horizontal cap of 8 that
    @ref movement-accuracy-ground-applied describes. It needs the same exemption a sucker tube has.
-   The step is applied **after** the brake, not before. The original's ramp reads 2.0, 3.878, 5.756 and
    then exactly 6.000, and those middle values are a step of half the strength *minus* one friction step
    of 0.122. Applying it where the floor-event handler naturally runs - before the brake - leaves the
    steady state one friction step short, at 5.862.
-   Leaving the belt **clamps to the walk cap**, exactly as leaving a tube does. Without that the player
    keeps the belt speed and coasts: 560 px past the end of a strength-8 belt against the original's 80.

Steady speed and cap are now exact at both strengths measured, and travel is within 1.4-3.7%. What is left
is the first two or three ticks of the ramp, where a per-frame reading is being compared against a per-tick
one and the acceleration conversion makes ours a third of a tick ahead.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par Run makes the belt faster, and it is the belt that changes rather than the ending
Holding Run raises the speed the belt drives to, by a **flat** amount rather than a factor: 18 px/tick at
strength 4 and 24 at strength 8, against 6 and 12 with nothing held. That is the same 1.5 per unit shifted
by 12 --- or, equivalently, eight more strength, which is
@ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyAccBeltRunBonus. Two strengths were needed to see it, because one point
cannot tell 4 × 4.5 from 6 × 3 and the answer is neither; `bl_acc_right_p8_run` was added for exactly that.
Only the target moves. The ramp's step is unchanged --- what differs about the ramp is that the brake it is
applied after becomes the dash brake, reading 1.573 instead of 1.878.

The belt is also the one carry fast enough to show that the **exit clamp has two targets** rather than being
skipped while Run is held: both strengths snap from 18 and 24 to exactly **16** and then decay at the dash
brake, where with nothing held they snap from 6 and 12 to **4** and decay at the walk brake. A tube carries
8 px/tick and Spaz's kick leaves at 15.88, so the 16 clamp is inert at both --- which is why the sidekick's
measurement read as "Run skips the snap" and could not be generalised. See
@ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyCarryExitRunSpeed, which all three sites now share.

Riding the belt with Run held, the original also travels *less* than the speed it reports --- 10 px/tick
while reporting 18, and 16 while reporting 24 --- which was recorded here for a long time as unfittable,
because those two points alone cannot tell a cap from a subtraction of 8. The **ramp** is what tells them
apart, and it was in the trace all along: on `bl_acc_right_run` the travel follows `xs` exactly while it
climbs, 5.145, 6.719, 8.291, 9.863, and only then flattens at 10 while `xs` carries on to 18. A subtraction
would have started at 10 and it does not, so it is a ceiling.

One ceiling then fits all four combinations --- **the belt's own strength plus the walk cap**, with the Run
bonus left out:

Strength | Run | Reports | Travels | Ceiling @m_span{m-text m-dim} (strength + 4) @m_endspan
-------- | --- | ------- | ------- | -------
4 | --- | 6 | **6** | 10, never binds
8 | --- | 12 | **12** | 16, never binds
4 | held | 18 | **10** | **10**
8 | held | 24 | **16** | **16**

@m_class{m-noindent}

So Run raises what the belt drives to by twelve and what it actually moves the player by four. Ours was
fully exempt from the applied cap and travelled the whole reported speed.

@subsection movement-accuracy-objects-oneway A one-way floor is passable to the body and solid to the jump

Nearly all of this was already right. Both games let a rising player straight through a one-way platform and
both catch a falling one on it, frame for frame: on `ow_hop`, a hop from the platform at row 33 that peaks
just above the one at row 31, the two pass through at the same tick and come to rest 2 px apart. Holding
Down on one does not drop the player through in either game, and neither does holding it through the whole
fall onto one --- `ow_down` and `ow_down_fall` both land and stay.

What differs is what the **jump** test makes of the tile. In the original the platform's own mask still
answers "is there ground under the feet", so crossing one with the jump key held launches a fresh jump. A
ladder of them can therefore be climbed by simply holding the key, and that is measurable: on `ow_jump` the
original's rise resets to exactly −10 as the feet cross rows 31 and 29, two extra launches where this
engine took one and stopped four tiles up.

Quantity | Before | Original (70 Hz) | Now | Before was off by | Now off by
-------- | ------ | ---------------- | --- | ----------------- | ----------
Rise through one | passes | passes | passes | exact | exact
Fall onto one | lands | lands | lands | exact | exact
Down while standing on one | stays | stays | stays | exact | exact
Down through the fall onto one | lands | lands | lands | exact | exact
Jump held, climbing the ladder | 1 launch, row 29 | **7 launches, row 10.45** | row 10.51 | 4.5 tiles short | 2 px

@m_class{m-noindent}

The last row is `ow_hold`, and it is the scenario the rule should be read off. Every other one here releases
the jump key at a fixed tick, and a release that falls within a tick or two of a crossing is worth a whole
jump's height --- while this engine's scripted input runs about two ticks behind the original's throughout,
which is exactly that margin. An earlier `ow_jump` let go at tick 50 with the original crossing at 49 and
this engine at 52, and read as a 50 px error that was nothing of the kind. Both releases are now kept well
clear of a crossing, and with the key never released at all the two climbs agree to 2 px over seven rungs.

Two things about the implementation are deliberate and neither is obvious.

@ref Jazz2::Actors::ActorState::CanJump cannot carry this. It is cleared outright for **any** actor with
@cpp _speed.Y < 0 @ce, and it is also what selects the ground-bound slope path in
@ref Jazz2::Actors::ActorBase::TryMoveSubstep(), so setting it while rising would have the slope search snap
the player back down onto the platform they are passing through. The question is asked separately, by
@ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::IsOnLegacyOneWayFloor(), and only the relaunch reads it. That probe asks the
same box the grounded test uses --- just below the feet --- **twice**, once with @cpp Downwards @ce and once
without, and a surface that is solid only in the first answer is a one-way and nothing else. Asking once
would also catch the real floor a jump has just left, whose feet are still inside that box for a frame or
two, and relaunch every frame: an unbounded rise from any ordinary standing jump.

And the relaunch runs from the **end** of the update rather than from
@ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::HandleJump(), because the original asks the question of the position its move
ended on. There is no @cpp _jumpTime @ce gate on it: the original's gaps between successive launches are 7,
9 and 12 ticks, and that cooldown is 10 frames --- 11.7 ticks --- so honouring it would swallow the first
two. A cooldown is not what limits this in the original; the spacing of the platforms is.

@subsection movement-accuracy-objects-poles Poles obey two different laws

-   **Vertical: additive.** @cpp 15.625 + abs(entry) @ce, slope exactly 1.0, fitted over entry speeds
    6.6 → 24.5. A pole hands back everything it was given plus a fixed bonus.
-   **Horizontal: multiplicative, floored and clamped.** @cpp clamp(3 * abs(entry), 8, 20) @ce, fitted on
    0 → 8, 4 → 12, 9.34 → 20, 16 → 20.

@m_class{m-noindent}

This is why poles **compound**, which is what exposed the earlier flat-value model as a regression.
Measured heights against a single pole's 268.6 px:

Setup | Height | Launch
----- | ------ | ------
2 poles chained | 440.6 px @m_span{m-text m-dim} (13.8 tiles) @m_endspan | −23.375
3 poles | 611.1 px @m_span{m-text m-dim} (19.1 tiles) @m_endspan | −24.5
5 poles | 951.4 px @m_span{m-text m-dim} (29.7 tiles) @m_endspan | −26.75
Blue spring → 1 pole | **804.5 px** @m_span{m-text m-dim} (25.1 tiles) @m_endspan | −40.125

@m_class{m-noindent}

A pole takes ~140 ticks from grab to launch, which is why chains take so long to play out --- and why the
probe gives pole scenarios a much longer window than the rest.

@m_class{m-block m-danger}

@par The horizontal pole's floor, and why it took a level to find
Three of the four fit points above are clamped or near it, and for a long time only three were known ---
4 → 12, 9.34 → 20, 16 → 20. Since the last two sit on the ceiling, **only one point was actually
determining the curve**, and @cpp 3*v @ce, @cpp 2*v + 4 @ce and @cpp 4*v - 4 @ce all pass through it. They
disagree completely at the bottom of the range, where they extrapolate to 0, 4 and −4, and the fit was
recorded as exact anyway.

The case that separates them is an entry speed of exactly **zero** --- a player who *drops* onto a pole
rather than running into one --- and no scenario made of props ever produced it, because every one of them
approaches a pole by moving towards it. It turned up the first time the probe was pointed at a hand-built
spring-and-pole chain in the test level, where a fall lands on a horizontal pole with the horizontal speed
already spent against a wall. Measured on both sides, three ways round (dropped in from the chain, and
jumping straight up into an overhead pole facing each way):

Entry | Original launch | Ours before | Ours now
----- | --------------- | ----------- | --------
0 | **−8.0** | 0.0 | −8.0
4 | +12.0 | +12.0 | +12.0
16 | +20.0 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (clamped) @m_endspan | +20.0 | +20.0

@m_class{m-noindent}

So the launch never returns less than 8, and at zero it goes **left** --- not in the direction the entry
speed's sign suggests, and not the way the player is facing either: facing right, having arrived from the
right, the original still launches left. Both halves matter, and they are one line each:
@ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyHPoleLaunchBonus and a strictly-greater comparison on the entry force.

@m_class{m-block m-success}

@par …and the floor turned out to be the whole rule
A fifth entry speed settled it, and it is the one the three candidates disagreed about most. `ob_hpole`
enters at a **run-up that never reaches the dash cap** --- 9.89 px/tick, squarely in the middle of the
range --- and the original launches at **17.8877**. All three multiplies predict the clamped 20 there.
`v + 8` predicts 17.8877 exactly, and it reproduces the other four points as well: 0 → 8, 4 → 12,
17.888 → 20 and 20 → 20, the last two capped. So the horizontal pole is the vertical one's rule
@m_span{m-text m-dim} (@ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyPoleLaunchBonus, `|entry| + 15.625`) @m_endspan
with a smaller bonus and a ceiling, and the "floor" was never a separate rule --- it is what `v + 8`
returns when `v` is zero. One constant replaced three.
@par
Reading that fifth point needs the entry-speed caveat below applied: the original logs the speed *before*
the tick's acceleration, so the grab tick reads 9.5215 and the pole saw one dash step (0.3662) more. Taken
at face value the rule looks approximate, at 17.52 against 17.888; corrected it is exact. The reason this
point existed all along and was never used is that `ob_hpole` is measured by **travel**, and both games end
it against the same wall --- 3636.9 against 3636.8 --- so a 12% error in the launch left no trace in the
figure anyone was reading.

Without them the engine **sticks on the pole for ever**. A launch of zero leaves the player inside the
pole's own tile; @cpp _lastPoleTime @ce excludes that tile for 10 ticks, the player has not moved by then,
and it re-grabs --- one grab every 70 ticks, indefinitely. That is exactly what a player reported, and the
same layout in the original runs the chain through without ever sticking.

@subsection movement-accuracy-objects-poleascent What a pole launch decays at

Porting the chain scenarios turned up a difference that had been hiding in plain sight: the ascent off a
pole was nearly three times too long, so a two-pole chain rose 772 px against the original's 440. Reading
the original's trace tick by tick settles it --- the launch is −22.25 and the rise decays by **exactly
0.875 a tick over 25 consecutive ticks**, which is the *released* rise gravity, even though jump is held
for the whole scenario. Applied travel sits at 8.00 a tick throughout, the cap doing its usual job.

This engine used 0.375, the held rate. Since a chained pole is entered with whatever survives the previous
ascent, the error compounded down the chain. The pole launch now hands the ascent over in the released
state:

Setup | Original | Before the ascent fixes | Now
----- | -------- | ----------------------- | ---
1 pole | 270.4 px | 480.7 | **270.6**
2 poles | 440.6 px | 772.6 | **442.7**
3 poles | 611.1 px | 932.5 | **614.8**
5 poles | 951.4 px | 529.2 | 1009.0 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (+6.1%) @m_endspan
Blue spring → 1 pole | 804.5 px | 539.4 | **806.3**

@m_class{m-noindent}

The spring-fed case is the one the *arrival* fix above settles --- it was a third short and is now within
0.2%. The five-pole chain is the residual: every individual launch matches, and the entry speeds converge
to 0.85% by the fifth pole, so the 6.1% is accumulated grab timing rather than a wrong figure. Note the
earlier 1011.2 px reference for it was contaminated by a pole-attached start; 951.4 is the clean one.

@m_class{m-block m-info}

@par It does not generalise - a spring is the other way round
The obvious reading is "a launch the player did not ask for decays at the released rate", and the RF blast
and the bounce off a stomped enemy both support it. A **spring** does not: measured, its ascent holds the
launch speed for two ticks and then decays at 0.375, the *held* rate. So this is per launch source, and the
only way to know which is to read the trace.

And it is not even per launch *type*: the same pole gives 0.875 reached by jumping and 0.375 reached off a
spring, so what a pole hands back depends on how the player arrived at it. Four sources measured, three
answers, one of them conditional --- there is no rule here to infer, and every attempt to state one has been
wrong. Read the decay off the trace tick by tick; the differences are exact multiples of 0.125 and show up
immediately, while the *height* they produce takes 20 ticks to diverge visibly.

@m_class{m-block m-danger}

@par Which means a spring has to *say so*, and for a while it did not
Because the rate is chosen by @cpp _jumpReleased @ce, and a spring that leaves that flag alone inherits
whatever the player's last jump left in it. A spring is also not a solid object, so descending onto one
never calls @cpp OnHitFloor() @ce to clear it. Land on a blue spring straight out of a released jump ---
dropping into a shaft, or just tapping jump on the way down, which for Jazz also starts a copter --- and
the whole ascent ran at 0.875: **399 px instead of 602**, from the same spring, decided by something the
player did before touching it. The user found it as a spring that "sometimes has half strength", and it was
invisible to the sweep because the probe's spring scenarios press nothing at all, so the flag was always
already clear. @cpp OnHitSpring() @ce now clears it explicitly, and @cpp jr_spring_fall @ce /
@cpp jr_spring_fall_jrel @ce guard it --- two identical drops onto one spring that differ only in a jump
tap, so their rises must match. Note @cpp _isSpring @ce already stopped a *later* release from shortening
the rise; the stale one was the gap. The probe now also logs @cpp jrel @ce and @cpp spr @ce next to every
row, because which rate an ascent picked does not show in the position for several ticks.

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par The speed-distribution check is blind to a pole
Every single-pole scenario passed the routine comparison while rising 78% too far, because a pole scenario
spends about 140 ticks frozen on the pole at zero speed and those zeros dominate the distribution. It is
the same failure as comparing endpoints, in a new disguise: a metric that ignores *when* things happen also
ignores a phase that happens to be long and uneventful. Read the launch and the rise directly for anything
involving a pole.

@m_class{m-block m-danger}

@par The five-pole reference was contaminated, and by the probe's own rest check
The original's five-pole trace began with the player frozen three tiles up and launching at tick 10, before
its own scripted jump at tick 45 --- it was hanging on the *previous* scenario's pole. Clearing the tile
events earlier did not help, because the settle decides the player has come to rest by watching the
position go quiet, and **a player hanging on a pole is perfectly stable**. It passed on the first tick
every time. Requiring the player to be back at the recorded ground height fixes it, and the threshold has
to be tight: a pole at the ground tile centre sits only 3.5 px above the resting height, so an 8 px window
still let it through. Both probes now check it at 1.5 px.

The clean reference is **951.4 px, not the 1011.2** that had been quoted. And once both sides are read
launch by launch rather than as a total height, the chain turns out to agree closely --- the earlier
"17% shortfall" was the *same* contamination on this engine's side, in a trace captured before its own
settle check was tightened, where the five-pole scenario began 98.5 px above the ground:

Pole | Original launch | Ours | Difference
---- | --------------- | ---- | ----------
1 | −22.250 | −21.246 | 1.004
2 | −23.375 | −22.565 | 0.810
3 | −24.500 | −23.884 | 0.616
4 | −25.625 | −25.203 | 0.422
5 | −26.750 | −26.522 | 0.228

@m_class{m-noindent}

The original's launches grow by exactly +1.125 a pole; ours by +1.319, so the two *converge* and by the
fifth pole they are 0.85% apart. The pole-to-pole spacing is identical at 160 px, and the final free
ascent measures 205 px here against 204 there, decaying at the same 0.875 with the same 8 px applied cap.
So the law, the bonus and the ascent are all right.

What is left is **when the pole is grabbed**, and it is visible on the very first one. The original jumps
at −10, rises with the held gravity of 0.375 and the usual 8 px applied cap, and at the tick before the
grab it is at y = 1253.75 doing −6.625; the next tick it is snapped to y = 1231.00, the pole tile's centre,
and 6.625 is the entry speed that goes into the launch. This engine grabs about a tick later --- 5.7 px
higher up the pole and at **5.62** rather than 6.625 --- so a *single* pole launches 4.5% weak. Each
further link then recovers a little of it, which is why a five-chain ends up much closer than one pole
does. Correcting it means moving when the pole event is detected relative to the player's hitbox, which is
shared event-detection geometry rather than a movement constant.

@subsection movement-accuracy-objects-push Push speed was frame-rate dependent

3.8.0 computed @cpp pushSpeedX * 1.2 * timeMult @ce --- scaling a *speed* by the frame delta. That made
pushing **2.5× faster at 24 FPS than at 60**. Measured: a flat 0.375, player and object together, and
<b>Run makes no difference</b> @m_span{m-text m-dim} (walking into a box and dashing into a rock push at
exactly the same rate) @m_endspan. This was the one genuine frame-rate bug the whole exercise turned up.

@section movement-accuracy-special Special moves

Quantity | 3.8.0 | Original (70 Hz) | Now (60 Hz) | How far 3.8.0 was off | How far Now is off
-------- | ----- | ---------------- | ----------- | --------------------- | ------------------
Buttstomp descent | 10.967 | 10.0 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (all three characters) @m_endspan | 11.6667 | −6.0% | exact
Buttstomp descent, *held* | *(accelerated)* | governed: 10.0 every tick | 11.6667 every tick | ran away to the fall cap | exact
Buttstomp sideways drift | @cpp 0.2 * movement @ce, ×2.6 Run | 0.18311, **×2** Run | 0.21362 / 0.42725 | −6.4%, Run ×1.3 too strong | exact

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par The descent is governed, not launched
Read off a long drop, the original's @cpp ys @ce sits at exactly 10.0 for **every tick** of the descent. The
transition callback here assigned the speed once and turned gravity back on, so the stomp accelerated away
from it --- fine on a stomp entered from a short hop, which is why it passed for so long, but a stomp entered
from a long fall reached the ordinary fall cap of 14.0 and hit whatever was below it 20% too hard. The cap in
@cpp OnUpdatePhysics() @ce is now @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyButtstompSpeed while a stomp is active
rather than @ref Jazz2::Actors::Player::LegacyFallSpeedCap, which holds it: measured 700.0 px/s against the
original's 700.2, and now independent of the drop height, as a governed descent has to be.
Copter descent | 1.5 | 1.0 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (Jazz = Lori) @m_endspan | 1.16667 | +28.6% | exact
Copter horizontal | *(unrestricted)* | walk accel and walk cap | same | *(uncapped)* | exact
Uppercut launch | −2.4127 + external force −1.4476 | **−6.59375 of travel** | −7.6927 | wrong mechanism | exact
Uppercut gravity while driving | *(ordinary rise gravity)* | 0.03125 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (`2048/65536`) @m_endspan | 0.042535 | <b>~12× too heavy</b> | exact
Uppercut driven length | *(until the rise decayed to −2)* | 30 ticks | 25.7 frames | *(absent)* | exact
Uppercut height @m_span{m-text m-dim} (end to end) @m_endspan | 189.4 px | 228.3 px | 228.5 px | −16.4% | +0.1%
Spaz double jump launch | −0.6 + internal force −1.15 | **−8.0** | −9.3333 | wrong mechanism | exact
Spaz double jump horizontal | @cpp clamp(x * 0.4, -1, 1) @ce | **0** | 0 | damped, not dropped | exact
Spaz double jump window | *(none)* | falling, **≤ 29 ticks after the release** | ≤ 25.7 frames | *(absent)* | exact
Spaz sidekick speed | 14.4 | 16.0 | 18.6667 | −22.9% | exact
Spaz sidekick driven distance | ~1728 px @m_span{m-text m-dim} (timeout 120) @m_endspan | **440 px**, then coasts to 504 | 440 px, coasts to 503.2 | **+243%** | −0.3%
Lori kick speed | 9.3 flat | <b>@cpp (n/2)² @ce ramp</b>, peak 42.25 | ramp, peak 49.29 | wrong shape | exact
Lori kick distance | ~372 px @m_span{m-text m-dim} (timeout 40) @m_endspan | **204.75 px** | 204.8 px | +82% | +0.02%
Lori kick period | *(input-paced)* | 45 ticks | 44--45 ticks | *(absent)* | −2% to 0%
Special move asked for mid-slide | full uppercut/sidekick | **refused**, an 8 px hop then a buttstomp | refused, 3.5 px hop then a buttstomp | 201 px of rise, 700 px of kick | hop −56%
Shot fired while looking up, sliding | fires | fires | fires | *(agrees)* | *(agrees)*
Kick against a pushable, Lori | *(stops dead)* | 4.5 px per kick, 12 ticks | 4.7 px, 12 ticks | **no push at all** | +4%
Kick against a pushable, Spaz | *(stops dead)* | 19.1 px, 51 ticks | 21.2 px | **no push at all** | +11%
Pushed object's own rate | 0.4286 @m_span{m-text m-dim} (a per-frame 0.5) @m_endspan | **0.375**, the player's own | 0.375 | +14% | exact
RF blast throw speed | *(one-off force 4)* | **8.0** | 9.3333 | wrong mechanism | exact
RF blast hold before friction | *(none)* | 30 ticks | 25.7 frames | *(absent)* | exact
RF blast upward kick, airborne | *(none)* | 12.0 | 14.0 | *(absent)* | exact
